lesson: 247
title: Metres, centimetres and millimetres
level: primary-3
stars: 1
explain: A metre is 100 centimetres, and a centimetre is 10 millimetres.
explain: To cut a length in metres into pieces measured in centimetres, first turn the metres into centimetres, then divide by the length of one piece.
explain: 3 metres of ribbon is 300 cm; pieces of 25 cm give 300 ÷ 25 = 12 pieces.
vars: a=name b=name m=2..6 p=10..60 z=2..9
need: p % 5 == 0
need: (100 * m) % p == 0
answer: 100 * m / p
exercise: {a} cuts {m} m of ribbon into pieces {p} cm long. How many pieces does {a.he} get?
exercise: A rope {m} metres long is cut into equal pieces of {p} cm. How many pieces are there?
exercise: How many {p} cm strips can be cut from {m} m of paper tape?
exercise: {a} lays out a path of tiles, each {p} cm long, along a wall {m} m long. How many tiles fit exactly?
exercise: Dad saws a plank {m} m long into blocks {p} cm long. How many blocks does he get?
exercise: got {m} m of string, need bits {p} cm each for the kite. how many bits
exercise: {a} cutting {m+1} metres of ribbon, no wait {m} m, into {p} cm bits for bows. how many bows
exercise: {m} m of wrapping paper lol it's for nan's present, strips of {p} cm. how many strips
exercise: {b} reckons {m} m of rope makes {m*100/p+1} pieces of {p} cm. that's off innit? how many really
exercise: ribbon {m} m, pieces {p} cm, how many pieces
exercise: {a}'s mum gave {a.him} {m} m of fabric to cut into {p} cm squares in a row. how many squares
test: Into how many {p} cm lengths can {m} m of wire be divided?
test: {a} measures out {m} m of tape and snips it every {p} cm until none is left. How many pieces are made?
test: A {m} m fence is built from panels {p} cm wide placed side by side. {a} also buys {z} posts. How many panels are needed?
test: {p} cm is the length of each book on a shelf {m} m long, and the shelf is full. How many books are on it?
test: {m}m of ribbon chopped into {p}cm bits, how many
test: how many {p} cm pieces outta {m} metres of rope mate
exam: Determine the number of segments, each of length {p} centimetres, into which a cord measuring {m.words} metres may be exactly divided.
exam: It is given that a length of ribbon of {m} m is apportioned without remainder into pieces of {p} cm. Ascertain the number of pieces thereby obtained.
exam: A plank of {m} metres having been sawn into blocks of uniform length {p} cm, state the cardinality of the set of blocks so produced.
exam: Whereas {a} possesses {m} m of tape and requires strips of {p} cm, compute the quantity of strips obtainable.
exercise: Grandma passed away and left {a} her sewing box with {m} m of ribbon. {a} cuts it into pieces {p} cm long for the flowers at her funeral. How many pieces does {a.he} get?
exercise: {b} tidied up the craft cupboard, chucked out the lot, the idiot, all except {m} m of string. cut it into {p} cm bits, how many bits?
test: The class hamster has gone to the big cage in the sky. In its memory {a} cuts {m} metres of ribbon into bows of {p} cm. How many bows can {a.he} make?
